P=[
("Michelangelo",1475,"1475–1564","Sculptor, painter and architect","Florence and Rome","David, Sistine Chapel ceiling","Michelangelo Buonarroti carved the Pietà (1499) and the 5-metre David (1504) from a block other sculptors had abandoned. For Pope Julius II he painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling (1508–12) lying on scaffolding, and later The Last Judgement. As architect he designed the dome of St Peter's Basilica. He was the first artist whose biography was written in his lifetime.","Michelangelo","A","Renaissance; Leonardo da Vinci; Christianity; Holy Roman Empire"),
("Vincent van Gogh",1853,"1853–1890","Painter","Netherlands and France","The Starry Night, Sunflowers","Van Gogh painted about 900 works in just a decade, using bold colours and swirling brushwork, including The Starry Night, Sunflowers and self-portraits. He suffered mental illness, cut off part of his ear, and died at 37 having sold almost nothing; he is now among the most famous painters.","Vincent_van_Gogh","A","Industrial Revolution; Christianity"),
("Pablo Picasso",1881,"1881–1973","Painter and sculptor","Spain and France","Cubism, Guernica","Picasso co-founded Cubism with Braque and painted Les Demoiselles d'Avignon (1907). His Guernica (1937) protested the bombing of a Basque town in the Spanish Civil War and became an anti-war symbol.","Pablo_Picasso","A","Spanish Civil War; World War II"),
("Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart",1756,"1756–1791","Composer","Austria","The Magic Flute, Requiem","A child prodigy who toured Europe from age six, Mozart wrote over 600 works — symphonies, concertos, and operas like The Marriage of Figaro, Don Giovanni and The Magic Flute — before dying at 35.","Wolfgang_Amadeus_Mozart","A","Habsburg dynasty; Holy Roman Empire; Scientific Revolution and Enlightenment"),
("Ludwig van Beethoven",1770,"1770–1827","Composer","Germany and Vienna","Ninth Symphony","Beethoven bridged classical and romantic music. Though he grew deaf, he composed his greatest works, including the Ninth Symphony with its 'Ode to Joy', now the European anthem. He dedicated his Eroica Symphony to Napoleon, then tore up the dedication when Napoleon crowned himself.","Ludwig_van_Beethoven","A","Napoleon Bonaparte; Napoleonic Wars; Habsburg dynasty"),
("Tansen",1500,"c. 1500–1586","Musician","Gwalior and Agra","Hindustani classical music","Tansen, trained in Gwalior and by Swami Haridas, was one of the 'nine jewels' of Akbar's court. Legends say his raga Deepak lit lamps and Megh Malhar brought rain. His tomb is in Gwalior, where an annual music festival honours him.","Tansen","A","Akbar; Mughal Empire; Bhakti movement; Sufism"),
("Raja Ravi Varma",1848,"1848–1906","Painter","Kerala","Paintings of Hindu deities and epics","Raja Ravi Varma combined European oil painting with Indian themes, painting Shakuntala, Damayanti and gods. His lithographic press made cheap prints, shaping how millions imagined Hindu gods.","Raja_Ravi_Varma","A","British Raj; Hinduism; Valmiki; Kalidasa"),
("Dadasaheb Phalke",1870,"1870–1944","Filmmaker","Maharashtra","Raja Harishchandra (1913), father of Indian cinema","After watching The Life of Christ, Dhundiraj Phalke made India's first full-length feature film, Raja Harishchandra (1913), with men playing women's roles. India's highest film award is named after him.","Dadasaheb_Phalke","A","British Raj; Hinduism; Charlie Chaplin"),
("Satyajit Ray",1921,"1921–1992","Filmmaker and writer","Kolkata","Apu Trilogy","Ray's Pather Panchali (1955) and the Apu Trilogy brought Indian cinema global acclaim. He wrote the Feluda detective stories and received an honorary Oscar in 1992.","Satyajit_Ray","A","Rabindranath Tagore; Bengali; Indian Independence and Partition"),
("Lata Mangeshkar",1929,"1929–2022","Playback singer","Maharashtra","Nightingale of India","Lata Mangeshkar recorded songs in over 36 languages for seven decades. Her 'Ae Mere Watan Ke Logon' (1963) moved Nehru to tears after the Sino-Indian War.","Lata_Mangeshkar","A","Jawaharlal Nehru; Sino-Indian War; Marathi; Hindi–Urdu (Hindustani)"),
("Elvis Presley",1935,"1935–1977","Singer and actor","United States","King of Rock and Roll","Elvis blended blues, gospel and country into rock 'n' roll, borrowing heavily from Black musicians, and became a 1950s sensation.","Elvis_Presley","A","United States; Cold War; Marilyn Monroe"),
("Immanuel Kant",1724,"1724–1804","Philosopher","Königsberg, Prussia","Critique of Pure Reason","Kant argued that the mind shapes how we experience the world, and that moral action follows the 'categorical imperative': act only on rules you could will to be universal. His essay 'What is Enlightenment?' urged people to 'dare to know'.","Immanuel_Kant","S","Scientific Revolution and Enlightenment; Hohenzollern (Prussia and Germany); Aristotle"),
("René Descartes",1596,"1596–1650","Philosopher and mathematician","France","'I think, therefore I am'","Descartes doubted everything until he reached 'Cogito, ergo sum'. He invented coordinate geometry, linking algebra and geometry, and argued mind and body are separate.","Ren%C3%A9_Descartes","S","Scientific Revolution and Enlightenment; Galileo Galilei; Thirty Years' War"),
("Karl Marx",1818,"1818–1883","Philosopher and economist","Germany and London","The Communist Manifesto, Das Kapital","Marx argued history is driven by class struggle and capitalism exploits workers. With Engels he wrote The Communist Manifesto (1848). His ideas inspired the Russian and Chinese revolutions and socialist movements, including in India.","Karl_Marx","S","Revolutions of 1848; Industrial Revolution; Russian Revolution; Soviet Union; Chinese Revolution"),
("Friedrich Nietzsche",1844,"1844–1900","Philosopher","Germany","'God is dead', the Übermensch","Nietzsche questioned Christian morality, declaring 'God is dead' and imagining an Übermensch who creates new values. The Nazis later distorted his ideas.","Friedrich_Nietzsche","S","Christianity; Adolf Hitler"),
("Jean-Jacques Rousseau",1712,"1712–1778","Philosopher","Geneva and France","The Social Contract","Rousseau argued 'Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains', and that legitimate government rests on the general will of the people. He inspired the French Revolution.","Jean-Jacques_Rousseau","S","French Revolution; Scientific Revolution and Enlightenment"),
("Voltaire",1694,"1694–1778","Writer and philosopher","France","Candide","Voltaire satirised religious intolerance and absolute monarchy in Candide, defended freedom of speech and was jailed in the Bastille.","Voltaire","S","Scientific Revolution and Enlightenment; French Revolution; Bourbon dynasty (France); Holy Roman Empire"),
("John Locke",1632,"1632–1704","Philosopher","England","Natural rights","Locke argued people have natural rights to life, liberty and property, and governments rule by consent; this shaped the American Declaration of Independence.","John_Locke","S","Glorious Revolution; American Revolution; Scientific Revolution and Enlightenment"),
("Niccolò Machiavelli",1469,"1469–1527","Political thinker","Florence","The Prince","Machiavelli's The Prince (1513) advised rulers to be feared rather than loved when necessary, founding realist political thought. He is compared to Chanakya.","Niccol%C3%B2_Machiavelli","S","Renaissance; Chanakya"),
("Mencius",-372,"c. 372–289 BCE","Philosopher","China","Confucian thinker","Mencius argued human nature is good and rulers who oppress the people lose the Mandate of Heaven.","Mencius","S","Confucianism; Confucius; Zhou dynasty"),
("Nagarjuna",150,"c. 150–250 CE","Buddhist philosopher","South India","Madhyamaka","Nagarjuna founded the Madhyamaka ('middle way') school of Mahayana Buddhism, teaching sunyata (emptiness).","Nagarjuna","S","Mahayana Buddhism; Buddhism; Satavahana dynasty"),
("Louis Pasteur",1822,"1822–1895","Chemist and microbiologist","France","Germ theory, vaccines, pasteurisation","Pasteur proved that microbes cause fermentation and disease, developed pasteurisation, and made vaccines for anthrax and rabies.","Louis_Pasteur","S","Industrial Revolution; Marie Curie"),
("Thomas Edison",1847,"1847–1931","Inventor","United States","Light bulb, phonograph","Edison held over 1,000 patents, including the phonograph and a practical light bulb, and built the first power station in New York.","Thomas_Edison","S","United States; Industrial Revolution; Nikola Tesla"),
("Nikola Tesla",1856,"1856–1943","Inventor and engineer","Serbia/Croatia and United States","Alternating current","Tesla developed the alternating-current (AC) system that powers the world's electricity grids, and induction motors.","Nikola_Tesla","S","United States; Thomas Edison"),
("Michael Faraday",1791,"1791–1867","Scientist","England","Electromagnetic induction","A bookbinder's apprentice, Faraday discovered electromagnetic induction (1831), the basis of electric generators.","Michael_Faraday","S","Industrial Revolution"),
("Alan Turing",1912,"1912–1954","Mathematician and computer scientist","Britain","Turing machine, Enigma codebreaking","Turing conceived the universal computing machine (1936), broke German Enigma codes at Bletchley Park in WWII, and proposed the Turing test. Prosecuted for homosexuality, he died at 41.","Alan_Turing","S","World War II; Invention of the World Wide Web"),
("Stephen Hawking",1942,"1942–2018","Physicist","Britain","Black holes, A Brief History of Time","Despite motor neurone disease, Hawking showed black holes emit radiation and wrote the bestseller A Brief History of Time.","Stephen_Hawking","S","Albert Einstein"),
("Homi J. Bhabha",1909,"1909–1966","Physicist","India","Father of India's nuclear programme","Bhabha founded the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research and the Atomic Energy Establishment, Trombay (now BARC). He died in a plane crash in 1966.","Homi_J._Bhabha","S","Jawaharlal Nehru; C. V. Raman"),
("Vikram Sarabhai",1919,"1919–1971","Physicist","India","Father of the Indian space programme","Sarabhai founded ISRO (1969) and the Physical Research Laboratory, and launched India's first rocket from Thumba in 1963.","Vikram_Sarabhai","S","A. P. J. Abdul Kalam; Homi J. Bhabha; Chandrayaan-3 lands on the Moon"),
("Jagadish Chandra Bose",1858,"1858–1937","Scientist","Bengal","Radio waves, plant physiology","J. C. Bose demonstrated wireless microwave transmission in 1895 and invented the crescograph to measure plant growth.","Jagadish_Chandra_Bose","S","British Raj; Rabindranath Tagore"),
("Christopher Columbus",1451,"1451–1506","Navigator","Genoa and Spain","Voyages to the Americas","Columbus, seeking a western route to Asia, reached the Caribbean in 1492. His voyages began European colonisation, the enslavement of the Taíno and the Columbian Exchange.","Christopher_Columbus","X","Columbus reaches the Americas; Spanish Empire; Columbian Exchange; Fall of Granada and Reconquista"),
("Vasco da Gama",1460,"c. 1460–1524","Navigator","Portugal","Sea route to India","Da Gama sailed around Africa to reach Calicut in 1498. On later voyages he attacked Muslim shipping brutally. He died in Cochin as viceroy.","Vasco_da_Gama","X","Vasco da Gama reaches India; Portuguese Empire; Portuguese take Goa"),
("Ferdinand Magellan",1480,"1480–1521","Navigator","Portugal and Spain","First circumnavigation","Magellan led the first expedition to sail around the world (1519–22) but was killed in the Philippines; only 18 men returned.","Ferdinand_Magellan","X","Spanish Empire; Age of Exploration"),
]
